Task: Derive days available from dates (Phase 2, Tier 1/2)
Track: fy_tax_pack_20260729
INSERTED TASK - user feedback raised at the Phase 2 checkpoint: "Where we
ask 'Days available for rent' can we supply a date and auto calculate it.
For example my property was available from 22/11/2025."

SUMMARY
Availability is captured as dates and the day count derived, replacing
direct entry of the number.

FILES
- supabase/migrations/059_property_fy_facts_availability_dates.sql (new)
  adds available_from / available_to + a range check
- lib/tax/fy.ts - added daysAvailableInFy
- tests/lib/tax/fy.test.ts - 11 new tests (52 total)
- app/actions/property-fy-facts.ts - derives the count server-side
- components/property-fy-facts-panel.tsx - date inputs, live computed count
- lib/supabase/database.types.ts, tax-report page.tsx - wiring

WHY
The owner knows the date, not the count. Deriving 221 days from
22 Nov 2025 across a year boundary is error-prone by hand, and once only
the number is stored the figure cannot be checked or re-derived.

Storing the dates keeps the evidence behind the number - the same
recorded-fact-vs-derived-value distinction the tax pack has to preserve
elsewhere.

days_available_for_rent is RETAINED as the resolved value apportionment
reads, computed from the dates when supplied. Phase 3 therefore consumes
one field and need not know whether it came from dates or was entered
directly (the latter still supported for a year with several separate
availability windows).

Clamping semantics: the range is clamped to the FY, so dates outside the
year are permitted and meaningful - a property available since 2019 is
recorded as such and reports the full year. Only an inverted range is
rejected, by a named check constraint.

ATO NUANCE
Labelled "when it went on the market, not when a tenant moved in".
Availability precedes tenancy under the ATO test - the user noted their
property "was listed earlier", which is exactly this distinction.

VERIFICATION PERFORMED
- Red/green: 11 new tests, covering the reported case (22 Nov 2025 -> 221
  days), null start/end, clamping on both sides, closed ranges, a single
  day, zero when the range misses the year entirely, an inverted range,
  29 February in a leap FY, and a guard that the result never exceeds
  daysInFy.
- CI=true npm test -> 52 tests passed.
- npm run db:reset -> 59 migrations replayed cleanly.
- Live round-trip, with an INDEPENDENT cross-check: Postgres computed
  (date '2026-06-30' - available_from + 1) = 221, matching the TypeScript
  helper exactly. Cross-checking against the database's own date
  arithmetic rules out a shared bug in my own day-count logic.
- Constraints: start date before the FY allowed (2019 -> 365 days);
  inverted range -> property_fy_facts_available_range_check;
  equal from/to -> 1 day.
- npm run build -> Compiled successfully.

